WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.560
"That's recording on that camera, Tom."

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:03.710
Lovely! All right.

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:08.040
Hello, I'm Tom Scott, and I've got a story
for you.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:13.330
It's set in a future. Not necessarily the
future, I'm not saying this is what will happen,

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:17.019
but I think there's a plausible path from
where we are now to where we will be.

00:00:17.019 --> 00:00:20.109
And where we will be is April 2022. Ten years'
time.

00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:22.589
First person we're going to meet there is
Jason Stewart.

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:26.999
He's the MP for Tooting and Streatham. He
is the Minister for Social Development.

00:00:26.999 --> 00:00:28.589
Considered kind of a hotshot among his party

00:00:28.589 --> 00:00:32.730
after winning his seat in the 2020 General
Election in a very hard-fought contest.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:37.430
Also embroiled in a massive scandal, but we'll
get back to that in a minute.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:38.699
This is the 2022 iPhone. Looks about the same,

00:00:38.699 --> 00:00:41.109
the laws of physics dictate you still need
a battery brick,

00:00:41.109 --> 00:00:43.780
but what has changed is the mobile network
speed.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.340
5G now blankets the country in hundred-megabit
internet access,

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.620
and data caps are a thing of the past.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.980
Which means that last year, Apple reused a
trademark and introduced iLife.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.679
It's quite similar to Your History on Microsoft
Nokia phones,

00:00:56.679 --> 00:01:00.749
or Droid Locker on Android. They're all descendents
of the accountability systems

00:01:00.749 --> 00:01:04.269
that have been used for police officers and
care workers for years now.

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:07.180
With iLife, the phone is always recording
audio

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.340
and sending it and your location to a cloud
server.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.390
The descendents of Siri then generate a transcript
of it.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:16.470
If you're talking to someone else with iLife,
it'll give you a text chat log,

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.439
it's not perfect, but it's close enough.

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:23.220
And if you have the new Apple headset, it's
got a couple of cameras embedded in it,

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.720
which are always recording video when they're
connected.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.020
That's uploaded, analysed, and stabilised
by the same systems.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.799
The cable, incidentally, still gets tangled
in knots every time you put it in your bag.

00:01:33.799 --> 00:01:36.320
What this means is that you can say to people,

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.520
'this is what I'm looking at right now', or
'this is what I just looked at'.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.509
It's all hooked up to social networks. This
is what people use it for.

00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:48.240
Justin Bieber's comeback tour was livestreamed
round the clock, and never went below 50,000 viewers.

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:53.439
What's more important, though, is that iLife
gives you a lifelog.

00:01:53.439 --> 00:01:56.250
A complete history of every person you've
talked to,

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.009
every friendly smile, every stranger you've
passed in the street.

00:01:59.009 --> 00:02:03.899
Your own memory is now indexed, augmented,
searchable, and your first 50 gigs of storage are free.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.869
And if you think that's farfetched, let's
remember Facebook is less than 10 years old.

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:13.010
Twitter is only six years old, and the iPhone
itself? Five years.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:18.170
We've gone from T9 text entry to full voice
dictation in half the time we're talking about here.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.800
Which brings us back to Jason Stewart. Jason
is a member of 'Cheshire Boys',

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.030
the site for old alumni of his public school.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.870
It runs on Disco, a fairly popular community
framework,

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:30.290
that unfortunately has some fairly major security
flaws,

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.099
including one that allows mass stealing of
passwords.

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:38.349
Let's go to Russia. Natalia Orlowski is there.
She is 15, she lives in an ex-Soviet tower block

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:40.170
and she doesn't get out much.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:43.550
She's running a bot that pulls in as many
of those passwords as possible.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:46.060
Not for anything particularly evil, she saw
what happened to the group

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.200
that charged porn to Putin's credit card last
year,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.099
but because she can, she takes all these passwords,
she bundles them together,

00:02:53.099 --> 00:02:56.280
and she puts them out on a Pastebin site.
Because she can.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.209
It's just another big leak, there've been
loads of them before,

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:02.720
and Facebook and all the networks will of
course lock everyone's account down

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:04.790
and require that they change their password
again.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:08.790
But in the meantime, lots of people are looking
through this list and one person

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:13.019
is going to notice 'tootingjason@gmail.com'.
His password is 'stewart9',

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:15.110
that's the same password he uses everywhere,

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.239
and someone goes 'hey! Let's log into his
iCloud!'.

00:03:17.239 --> 00:03:20.200
First thing they see is Apple's handy 'Is
This You?' feature.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.319
Jason Stewart can be seen in a few frames
of this walkby in Shoreditch.

00:03:23.319 --> 00:03:27.379
He wasn't recording, but his phone had his
location and facial recognition did the rest,

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:30.670
and it didn't take too much effort to find
out what he was doing there.

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:34.720
A few minutes later, this poison pen letter
arrives in

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.540
every single political editor and blogger's
inbox in the country.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:41.450
The Sun eventually runs with it first, after
all the blogs have made it public knowledge.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:45.610
And Jason Stewart's career is ruined.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:47.189
Except it's not.

00:03:47.189 --> 00:03:49.560
Because he's not committing a crime under
UK law.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.360
He's divorced, so he wasn't even committing
adultery.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.760
It's 2022. He decides he's going to go on
the offence.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.549
He gets up in Parliament and says that

00:03:56.549 --> 00:04:00.040
'our private lives should be private, and
we must investigate these hackers'.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:03.120
And he convinces the Met to investigate, which
they do by

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.860
filing a request with Apple for any lifelogs
that mentioned Jason Stewart

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.370
in the hours and minutes before the leak.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:13.549
And Apple agrees, because that argument got
settled years ago

00:04:13.549 --> 00:04:14.569
after Blackpool.

00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:21.450
So that's the way it works now. Someone's
arrested, sixteen years old, so they can't be named,

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:23.600
but we know they've been released on bail
on the grounds

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.710
that they not use the internet or a computer,
which means of course

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:28.470
that they can't do anything.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:32.990
And that's the precedent set. If you're in
the same area as the victim of a crime,

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:37.710
or you say the same name as a victim of a
crime in the minutes or hours before,

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:41.600
your lifelog will get pulled and searched
through by the police.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.620
And you can always close your account and
opt out, of course,

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.550
that option is right there, but it's so damned
useful.

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:51.070
And besides, all your friends use it.

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:54.380
And besides, lots of companies are requiring
that their employees use it

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.200
for liability purposes.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.990
And besides all that, it's not like you've
got anything to hide, right?

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.930
I'm Tom Scott. Thank you very much.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:03.350
(APPLAUSE)

